<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.14.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01506</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>701bdbda-dbff-4f63-ae48-44c187a9b614</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><section><exercise id="_91b86a46-3b99-4625-ad43-24e8495050c2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Functions \(a, b, c, d, e\), and \(f\) are given below. Which functions are linear?<emphasis>Select the three</emphasis> answers
          that apply.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.1. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is \(a(x)=3x\), \(d(x)=9+3x\), \( f(x)=9 \cdot 3x\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7e5c2385-363a-4bc2-b500-8771bcfa0add" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Functions \(a, b, c, d, e\), and \(f\) are given below. Which functions are exponential?<emphasis>Select the two</emphasis> answers that apply.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.1.</para><para>The correct answer is \(b(x)=3^x\), \(e(x)=9 \cdot 3^x\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c560e6be-58d4-4a05-94bd-22d2063c7a09" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Here are 4 equations defining 4 different functions, \(a, b, c\), and \(d\). List them in order of
          increasing rate of change. That is, start with the one that grows the slowest and end with the one that grows
          the quickest.
          <para>\(a(x)=5x+3\)</para>
            <para>\(b(x)=3x+5\)</para>
            <para>\(c(x)=x+4\)</para>
            <para>\(d(x)=1+4x\)</para>
         </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Option 1: \(a(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(c(x)\), \(d(x)\)</para><para>Option 2: \(d(x)\), \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(a(x)\)</para><para>Option 3: \(a(x)\),\(d(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(a(x)\)</para><para>Option 4: \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(d(x)\), \(a(x)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(d(x)\), \(a(x)\).</para></solution></exercise></section><para>Use the given information for problems 4 – 6. Technology is required. Function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=3x+5\),
  and function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=(1.1)^x\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
  <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>
     </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
        \(f(x)\)
     </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
        \(g(x)\)
     </emphasis> </entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        1
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        5
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        10
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        20
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>
<section><exercise id="_04eb6bed-b602-46eb-95d3-8bcd36a15239" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which values will complete the table for \(f(x)\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>8, 11, 14, 17</para><para>8, 20, 35, 65</para><para>3, 15, 30, 60</para><para>8, 15, 30, 60</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.2.</para><para>The correct answer is 8, 20, 35, 65.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="f8c05a03-2554-4d04-9263-88fb53e061e5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Which of the following sets of values will complete the table for \(g(x)\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>8, 20, 35, 65</para><para>1.1, 5.5, 10.10, 20.20</para><para>1.1, 1.6, 2.59, 6.73</para><para>1.1, 1.21, 1.33, 1.46</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.2. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is 1.1, 1.6, 2.59, 6.73.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_02e97ed5-02f5-4a77-affa-d83b7b8287f2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>
          <para>________ is a linear function.</para>
         </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(f(x)\) is a linear function.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e02206a3-611d-4186-b1af-4a9fffd02411" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>________ is an exponential function.</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(g(x)\) is an exponential function.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_242f0919-e0cc-46f6-906d-e500e5353468" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As \(x\) increases from
          0, __________ reaches 30 first.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is \(n(x)\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="a529212b-5978-4fb5-89a6-8c64dc239be0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As \(x\)<emphasis>
         </emphasis> increases from 0, __________ reaches 100 first.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is \(m(x)\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_274bbeb1-10b8-4f67-8aba-fcc075b326c9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The functions \(f\) and \(g\) are defined by \(f(x)=8x+33\) and \(g(x)=2 \cdot(1.2)^x\). Which function
          eventually grows faster, \(f\) or \(g\)? Explain how you know.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x)\) grows faster because 8 is greater than 1.2.</para><para>\(f(x)\) grows faster because 33 is greater than 2.</para><para>\(g(x)\) grows faster because it is a linear function.</para><para>\(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.</para></problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.4. 
     </para><para>The correct answer is \(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_479162ef-4347-4bc2-aa36-7089c8ada675" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A line segment of length \(l\) is scaled by a factor of 1.5 to produce a segment with length \(m\). The new
          segment is then scaled by a factor of 1.5 to give a segment of length \(n\).
          
          What scale factor takes the segment of length \(l\) to the segment of length \(n\)? 
       </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.1.</para><para>The correct answer is 2.25.</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
